#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230107} :activity/id #uuid "e5d881c7-3c4a-52c1-9e67-d3807291b86c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048817 :topic/id #uuid "c277f404-7746-5317-a99f-359354eb8c6a" :topic/math-academy-id 6682} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230184 :tutorial/content "Consider the integral\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^{b} f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the integration variable and $t$ is a parameter. How do we compute $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} I}{\\mathrm{d} t}$?\n\nIf $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b] \\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d} t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\mathrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as **differentiating under the integral sign**.\n\nThe integral represents the total area under the curve $y = f(x,t).$ Changing the parameter $t$ alters the height of the curve at each $x,$ but the interval $[a,b]$ remains fixed.\nThus, the total change in area is obtained by summing (integrating) how each point changes.\n\nFor example, if\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} e^{-tx}\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{d}{dt}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-tx}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}({e}^{-tx})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-x{e}^{-tx}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can then evaluate this integral using the usual methods. For the purposes of integration, we treat $t$ as a constant." :tutorial/id #uuid "2c1e693d-0af4-542d-87dd-e902e76bf8d7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14473 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d5bb1824-d10b-50d3-9fb2-1ad92235ca90" :step/math-academy-id 611854 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230108}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230108 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230220 :tutorial/content "Now, given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle I(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nlet's calculate $I'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb{R}$ by *differentiating under the integral sign*.\n\nRecall that if $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R,$ then the function\n\n$$\nI(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute $I'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\int_{-1}^{1} (3x-t^2)^2\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\int_{-1}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(3x-t^2)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-{t}^{2})^{2}] & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =2(3x-{t}^{2})\\cdot (-2t) \\\\  & =-4t(3x-{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-4t(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4t{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(3x-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}-{t}^{2}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0-2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-2{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =-4t(-2{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5875ac89-b808-53fa-b46e-33fadfb23647" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14657 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "86e6d79d-861a-515d-864d-c0329ef14eb6" :step/math-academy-id 611855 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230109}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230109 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229191 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228907 :question/id #uuid "301e7fe6-6892-5e0a-9368-b3857ae8fa39" :question/math-academy-id "e-21649"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050819}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229650} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229676} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308713} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308714} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321052} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444062} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497372}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"} :step/id #uuid "a87fd0a0-542a-56a3-9f60-3db5c26834a8" :step/math-academy-id 611856 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230197 :tutorial/content "Consider the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\qquad t \\in [c,d].\n$$\n\nThen, *differentiation under the integral sign* applies, meaning\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\int_{a}^b \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x,\n$$\n\nprovided the following conditions are met:\n\n- $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b] \\times [c,d],$ and\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to compute $I'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (2x+t^3)^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x, \\qquad t \\in [0,5].\n$$\n\nWe may differentiate under the integral sign provided that the function $f(x,t) = (2x+t^3)^2$ fits the necessary criteria. Let's check.\n\nFix $t_0 \\in (0,5)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,5],$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\n- Notice that $f(x,t) = (2x+t^3)^2$ is continuous on $R.$\n\n- Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = 6(2x+t^3)t^2,\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$\n\nThus, the operation is valid. We obtain\n\n$$\nI'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} 6(2x+t^3)t^2 \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "615f6015-058c-5a82-9f79-7f3c62982c3f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14538 :tutorial/title "Checking the Conditions"} :step/id #uuid "1469ff2b-3ce2-5cf7-a237-aea623f3e876" :step/math-academy-id 611859 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230111}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186230111 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229192 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228922 :question/id #uuid "830924ac-a51c-505a-b5b4-c8a4ad916dfe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048877}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311558}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"} :step/id #uuid "085c51cb-93b7-5cdd-ba82-cae0f0193721" :step/math-academy-id 611860}] :activity/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186229252}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444067} :activity/id #uuid "cf6c0bba-9840-5282-9c1e-6b17cae9759d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048817 :topic/id #uuid "c277f404-7746-5317-a99f-359354eb8c6a" :topic/math-academy-id 6682} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444064 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3937af2-b650-5eaa-a987-e97fd137f52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444058}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444058} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8e8dcc5-5048-58a4-a50f-532f28ef34ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6c38c2df-a1c4-591d-95c2-93cd739693be" :question/math-academy-id "q-334973" :question/problem "Given that $P(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${P}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${P}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${P}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nP(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}] & =2(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-1}^{1}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =0+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(t)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b5c3e8c-b479-5b7c-995d-1b489fe05efc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444069}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444066 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444063 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49bfc920-4798-5ac8-829c-10c6425543fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sin(t)-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444056}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444056} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4f3822a-92a6-5439-9a5d-bf0f8c9a91bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5b2ce271-5eac-5d80-b8db-22d863d22bcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-352887" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{4}3x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,x{|}_{0}^{4}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-24) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "187e39d4-f8d9-5968-b9f2-0918091fc0cc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444067 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba4b594e-6e46-5106-afac-33e43b94aed5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36t+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444057}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444057} :answer-field/id #uuid "831d751e-c629-5b32-9f44-368844ac1a78" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a7ae8c6-87fd-5aaa-b1ca-f181d86f7e93" :question/math-academy-id "q-334936" :question/problem "Given that $P(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${P}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${P}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${P}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nP(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-2}^{0}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-3t)^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-3t)^{2}] & =2(x-3t)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x-3t) \\\\  & =2(x-3t)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-6(x-3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}-6(x-3t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}(x-3t)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{-2}^{0}3t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{-2}^{0}-3tx{|}_{-2}^{0} \\\\  & =-2-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(t) & =-6(-2-6t) \\\\  & =12(3t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "452238fc-0a14-5395-9d29-80eea1eaebf3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444068 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333a10d9-6acd-51bc-a542-e5834510336a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466309} {:answer/id #uuid "0896e53d-ce4d-51df-a6c6-c42afcb5b091" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466310}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466310} :answer-field/id #uuid "d140fccf-9864-5e7c-ab64-46dce6612c96" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf441900-eba8-530f-9a51-60c9dde68b74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466311} {:answer/id #uuid "1b704fe9-df2a-5b5b-835b-671871eaa101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466312}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466311} :answer-field/id #uuid "97b4a9dc-d50b-57ee-a809-22e3de81aa9c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc749d3b-d1da-5e3f-96d4-b8e2ecc22be3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466313} {:answer/id #uuid "11dac17e-58fd-523c-9348-ca7babe92e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466314} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ebc44d1-abfd-536f-a11f-5b1f2580b68d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b7963456-3a78-5141-94e0-577dece1c8b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335249" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{3}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{2t{x}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ So, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{|x-t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,1)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that this function is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{t}^{2}).\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nSince $x\\in [0,1]$ and $t>0,$ we have $x+{t}^{2}>0,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2t}{x+{t}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "86e12b8a-080a-53fd-a5ee-62fcb2d5ac6b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444066}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444069 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b300e0b9-d29d-5f4a-b4c6-ddf029e8438c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466297} {:answer/id #uuid "048f2c66-c0d9-54d0-b540-10593e6a06b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466298}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466298} :answer-field/id #uuid "abb212ca-feab-55fb-bbe5-bf2eaadf951c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dbd657e-103b-5d31-b5c6-791565f66d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466299} {:answer/id #uuid "569d20a0-8036-5d36-bb7b-12d1ef5dc3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466300} :answer-field/id #uuid "eafc1e72-ec71-5219-9230-3bf6c0ee7ba2" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58b98bf5-3fd9-5b73-9571-b262aca2b590" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466301} {:answer/id #uuid "d7d11a7e-0e24-5812-8b81-e408a2fa4534" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466302}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466302} :answer-field/id #uuid "e69c2a12-6035-509f-9da1-9d85906ac437" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9de6d57f-11dc-5bb2-bebc-d38ef2773685" :question/math-academy-id "q-335247" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\nI. ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nII. ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nIII. ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "c87a8b02-417c-5ed5-80b6-82dec3b064cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444068}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444055}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495191} :activity/id #uuid "3fc4b6f4-448c-556a-9808-6a0a8e96a629" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048817 :topic/id #uuid "c277f404-7746-5317-a99f-359354eb8c6a" :topic/math-academy-id 6682} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308705 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6356d6f-89b1-53f4-9c09-ca1ebc669f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48{t}^{2}(4{t}^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186306852} {:answer/id #uuid "93728e3d-4732-5ef6-8a21-7fde64d07dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48t^2(4t^3-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494655}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494655} :answer-field/id #uuid "0cbc087d-b2a0-5c7f-9dde-4dc5f3d3b460" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "932e788a-5d6d-5848-ade5-e4094868063b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334931" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x-4{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x-4{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(x-4{t}^{3})\\cdot (-12{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}-24{t}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-24{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x-4{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{2}4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{2}-4{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2-8{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & =-24{t}^{2}(2-8{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =48{t}^{2}(4{t}^{3}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2682eeb7-9303-5555-aa81-d4f1b1bc53e1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495264}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495226 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21f7e4c1-6c8c-516f-99d4-3016aca7887a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466321} {:answer/id #uuid "a65ba9ef-0cc8-52d0-9799-3481656ed4a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466322}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466322} :answer-field/id #uuid "591c30be-8419-5dc6-80a9-275d90e3124a" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9372c8d-42b6-5923-978a-5855e094b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466323} {:answer/id #uuid "b5b3441a-93b7-54ca-a1d1-eb627c0c175b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466324}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466323} :answer-field/id #uuid "7308a380-c9c9-5971-af9f-58b1d0cea85c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467005} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d96d0868-95f2-538f-a7e5-d8a250db5ce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466325} {:answer/id #uuid "9df9f9c9-4956-531c-b65e-ed180dad9e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466326}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466326} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b3e4b44-d0ed-53af-8a31-aa805a851393" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2b175da7-6b72-569e-916d-da964786e33a" :question/math-academy-id "q-335252" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={e}^{-{t}^{2}x}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=-2tx{e}^{-{t}^{2}x},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{2t}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "90fefbca-de6e-5ca6-b465-c9d927b723d9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495246 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a4f09b1-f161-5622-a23d-b0ffdfb2d435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466303} {:answer/id #uuid "fcd5f489-5a61-5601-8ecc-98a5906dddc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466304}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466304} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e605653-b47a-5ef7-9a12-6a6e858e1bbf" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22edb3de-f55b-58a1-9692-099dbd00066f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466305} {:answer/id #uuid "581893f8-2232-5da0-be5a-6d7f06e1d7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466306}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466305} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c8b9f4c-0d1d-538d-9987-3817690edcc4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466996} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a13b11-4489-5b2f-bce4-6df115f00f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466307} {:answer/id #uuid "31dd6c34-6e6b-519e-82c4-6849b3fc2a97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466308}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466308} :answer-field/id #uuid "29ee4bc8-90a4-5de9-b5bb-ad229b502589" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "57d1f543-d099-580f-9280-d60da64177c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-335248" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{|x-t|}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in [0,1]$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)={x}^{2}t{e}^{-xt}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $0<c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{-xt}-{x}^{3}t{e}^{-xt}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{|x-t|}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is undefined when $x=t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, ${f}_{2}$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆[0,1],$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ because the denominator is always positive. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{2(x-t)}{{((x-t)^{2}+1)}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "b8e332a4-d8ae-569b-9c08-56ffd9814faa" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495226}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495264 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229676 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20fa6673-b649-516c-acab-67ebab0e5355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186305469} {:answer/id #uuid "6325146b-ac91-512c-88e2-d5557a7035bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+e^t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494519}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186494519} :answer-field/id #uuid "45913648-be5f-5c47-b51e-9b6361444fb4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50545819-8834-5efd-8519-92619f4fb053" :question/math-academy-id "q-334955" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{e}^{t})^{2}] & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{e}^{t}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{e}^{t})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{e}^{t}\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=2{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e52f37a8-a447-5f85-8c13-490937937ec2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186495246}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186494303}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497478} :activity/id #uuid "8b065634-803f-5739-9336-86488fa97262" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048817 :topic/id #uuid "c277f404-7746-5317-a99f-359354eb8c6a" :topic/math-academy-id 6682} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497438 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+3\\cos^{2}(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497037} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d913041-76e7-5e6e-a8c0-9a8b344b6d06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497244}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "74d3e4f5-83dd-544e-8625-a8bd48421738" :question/math-academy-id "q-352889" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{cos}^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({cos}^{2}⁡(t)-(-{cos}^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5f1e8c45-aac6-55f1-8386-0597586bbb27"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "817884f4-d5d2-5108-918e-d0ab3bb3b2ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20{t}^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319784} {:answer/id #uuid "86c9e8ab-6efc-512b-8e57-f76adb3a22c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20t^{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496936}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496936} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3fa6845-810a-5de2-b921-0cdbf047d2a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bb46a1e6-2768-5b65-8fa6-a9b43ac0ddde" :question/math-academy-id "q-350551" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}] & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5}) \\\\  & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot 5{t}^{4} \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{5}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1)+({t}^{5}-(-{t}^{5})) \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}(2{t}^{5})=20{t}^{9}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6b9aec71-5349-5890-880d-e9cca6269db7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497438}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311557 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "958e50e4-711b-576c-ab91-0087c75945cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466333} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8fed5f-0e8b-5ec1-8918-492f6fc7c6b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466334}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466333} :answer-field/id #uuid "5372760e-066f-55e0-b7b9-2ff11d55e2c0" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "832c18d4-aaf4-5e10-9aeb-143c9e0d4b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466335} {:answer/id #uuid "51ef8b21-0c2d-5d7e-bac0-f3e5033cf5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466336}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466336} :answer-field/id #uuid "1aeac474-d1d6-5a95-a8c4-6a2ce80af73e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467011} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "800c1d5b-5d46-5618-b8ef-1b9451c13e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466337} {:answer/id #uuid "4250fbe7-e751-5a5c-88fa-cbb83498f5e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466338}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466338} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7db462d-3fc8-5643-b099-4624769f01bf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "757f1645-7b5c-5866-81f6-7ae1eefa124d" :question/math-academy-id "q-335254" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{t}{1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}=\\frac{1+{x}^{2}-{t}^{2}}{(1+{x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{(x+t+1)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=-\\frac{3}{(x+t+1)^{4}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t\\in (0,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "7fea924a-9132-5c8e-8405-bb171905179c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497488}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497478 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497353 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ca15a3a-dee4-579f-a997-69fcee973c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3-2\\cos t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496861} :answer-field/id #uuid "624d06da-e063-516f-b272-d7b29fea68c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186497262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "41d2dc29-ff79-5299-80eb-91a0d11f7713" :question/math-academy-id "q-334970" :question/problem "Given that $J(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${J}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${J}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${J}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{J}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}] & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d4624cb3-279b-58e4-be04-71f50c9f1d95" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497451}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497488 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a22ab5cf-13df-5925-8c56-24a2e18fabaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466339} {:answer/id #uuid "ff310a56-8a6f-5ed7-bf98-61e53df7dc8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466340}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466339} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6103b29-3311-5514-bfba-8a71af2fd5aa" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467013} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ebac1c-34c4-5c6d-bb6c-785ac2249a6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466341} {:answer/id #uuid "945abe1f-1a4c-598a-b831-113ac0528de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466342} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbf27f1b-1445-5c81-aae4-f7f07eb27092" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467014} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9bbcda9-a787-548e-a44b-7fe7cb161bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466343} {:answer/id #uuid "cdb91fc8-2c32-5a9f-956a-db0acb298dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466344}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186466344} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cbdaacc-92a5-5c61-9124-f11f072369ac" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e7fa63bd-d15a-5bf7-b7e2-ddd1185810da" :question/math-academy-id "q-335255" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n- **I.** ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in (0,1)$\n- **II.** ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- **III.** ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{x}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t<0$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=|x-t|.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in (0,1)$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d$ such that $[c,d]⊆(0,1),$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ However, the partial derivative\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}={\\begin{aligned}-1,\\,x>t \\\\ 1,\\,x<t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ndoes *not* exist whenever $x=t={t}_{0},$ and is therefore not defined everywhere on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)={e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}\\in R$ and choose $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}={x}^{2}{e}^{t{x}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{x}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}<0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(-\\infty ,0)$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{x}{(x-t)^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all $t<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "fdb087f1-efc2-5793-8940-c3ea24f5ca43" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186497445}}] :activity/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496653}]}
